<h2>Where Wind Claims Get Disputed</h2>
<h3>Wind vs. wear and tear</h3>
<p>Insurers often say missing shingles reflect age, poor nailing, or failed sealant rather than wind. Creased tabs, the pattern of missing shingles across slopes, and documented wind speeds near the address all help answer that argument.</p>
<h3>Rain and wind-driven water</h3>
<p>Interior water is usually covered only when wind or hail created an opening first. If the insurer says rain came through an existing defect, the location of the opening and the timing of the damage matter.</p>
<h3>Tree removal and debris</h3>
<p>Many policies pay to remove a tree that damaged a covered structure, often with a per-tree or per-loss limit. A tree that fell in the yard without hitting anything may not be covered at all.</p>
<h3>Matching and partial repairs</h3>
<p>Replacing a few wind-torn shingles on an older roof may leave a mismatched patch, and brittle shingles can break during repair. How matching is handled depends on your policy language.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Downbursts, Straight-Line Winds, and Tornadoes</h2>
<p>For insurance purposes, the type of wind usually does not change coverage: a windstorm is a windstorm. It does change what the damage looks like. Downbursts push debris and trees in one direction across a wide path, while tornadoes leave a narrower, twisting path with debris thrown in several directions. Most Bexar County tornadoes on record were weak (F0/F1 or EF0/EF1), yet even a brief EF1, like the one that touched down in a Helotes subdivision on May 24, 2020 (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=891257" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI</a>), can lift roof decking and break windows. National Weather Service storm surveys, when one was done, are useful evidence for the date, path, and estimated wind speed.</p>

<h2>Wind Damage Across Greater San Antonio</h2>
<p>NOAA's 70 mph-plus wind reports are spread across the region: Leon Valley, Kirby, Converse, Alamo Heights, Helotes, Leon Springs, Elmendorf, and the far south side all appear in the record. Whether your property is in the city or in communities such as <a href="/service-areas/leon-valley/">Leon Valley</a>, <a href="/service-areas/converse/">Converse</a>, or <a href="/service-areas/alamo-heights/">Alamo Heights</a>, the same documentation steps apply. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lists what to gather.</p>

<h2>Deductibles and Coverage to Check</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind/hail deductible.</strong> Often a percentage of your dwelling limit. On smaller wind losses, the deductible may exceed the repair cost, so get an estimate before deciding how to proceed.</li>
<li><strong>Roof valuation.</strong> ACV-only roof endorsements or roof schedules reduce payment for older roofs. See <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Additional living expenses.</strong> If wind damage makes your home unlivable, loss of use coverage may help with temporary housing.</li>
<li><strong>Business income.</strong> Commercial policies may cover lost income during repairs. See <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster Helps With Wind Claims</h2>
<p>San Antonio Public Adjuster represents policyholders, not insurance companies. On a wind claim, we may inspect the roof and exterior, gather weather data for the date of loss, review your policy, prepare a line-item estimate in Xactimate, and communicate with the insurer's adjuster. If wind and hail struck together, we document both perils; see <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a>. When a claim has been paid low or denied, we can review the insurer's reasoning and discuss options such as a supplement or appraisal. Results depend on your policy and the facts; no one can guarantee an outcome. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>What to Do After a Windstorm</h2>
<ol>
<li>Stay clear of downed power lines and report them to CPS Energy.</li>
<li>Photograph damage, fallen trees, and debris before cleanup when it is safe.</li>
<li>Tarp openings and board broken windows; keep receipts.</li>
<li>Report the claim promptly and note the date and approximate time of the storm.</li>
<li>Keep damaged materials when practical until the insurer inspects.</li>
</ol>
<p>Many policies also set a contractual deadline to file suit after a claim dispute. If you have legal questions, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
